{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold","id":1242706,"title":"Mining and the Threshold","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-08-04T07:10:49-07:00","id":1242706,"title":"Mining and the Threshold","content":"So, this has always bugged me about the setting. Hopefully someone can point out something obvious about why it's not an issue.<br /> <br /> At various points in the source books, there are references to mining in the Threshold. But why wasn't it mined out in the First Age, or even earlier? Human civilisation has been ascendant for thousands of years. Did no-one use metal until the Age of Sorrows? What's left of the Threshold used to be the innermost part of Creation before the Fair Folk invasion. Even if we assume that, for some reason, the Solars exclusively mined in Wyld-Shaped areas, why didn't the Shogunate or later civilisations use it?<br /> <br /> Maybe there's an off-hand reference to the Bureau of Nature periodically renewing the ores of Creation somewhere, but if so I've missed it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24421-katamari_naneke","name":"Katamari_Naneke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-04T07:31:28-07:00","id":1242711,"title":"","content":"AFAIR, there were some mentions in 2nd ed in Harbourhead's Jade Mine that there was an elemental dragon that renewed the mine. Creation is a magical realm, and as such it follows that there are magical ways to get replenished resources. Those earth elementals need something to do, you know?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1524-pcontop","name":"pcontop","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1524_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-04T07:32:26-07:00","id":1242712,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Katamari_Naneke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold#post1242706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But why wasn't it mined out in the First Age, or even earlier?</div> </div> </div> </div>The First Age wasn't a period of industrialization in the manner of our modern Earth.<br /> <br /> \u200bDon't get me wrong, there are probably thousands of places littered around the setting in which the resources from below were exhausted centuries or millennia ago, but the world represented by Creation is plenty big enough for there to still be lots of untapped potential.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Katamari_Naneke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold#post1242706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Did no-one use metal until the Age of Sorrows?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you've been reading the book enough to catch the references to mines, you should also have seen that they used metals in the First Age; the write-ups for artifacts are pretty explicit about what they're made of.<br /> <br /> \u200bThat said, the First Age probably didn't have as great a demand for miners as you suspect; pacts with gods, earth elementals, and various entities from the Wyld could probably supply a not-insignificant bit of the metal needed for the miracles of the First Age without someone having to so much as swing a pickaxe. And while I'm sure metal <i>was</i> mined, the trade networks enabled didn't necessitate that such mines be absolutely everywhere.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Katamari_Naneke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold#post1242706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">why didn't the Shogunate or later civilisations use it?</div> </div> </div> </div>I know these days a bored billionaire can throw money to hire a dozen guys to pretty much <i>erase </i>a mountain to get at the shiny stuff under it, but historically, that sort of effort used to take a <i>lot</i> longer, with <i>vastly </i>more manpower and a <i>much </i>higher relative cost.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 08-04-2018, 12:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-04T21:20:53-07:00","id":1242864,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Katamari_Naneke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold#post1242706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe there's an off-hand reference to the Bureau of Nature periodically renewing the ores of Creation somewhere, but if so I've missed it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am under the impression that in 2e it was said that changes in the flows of elemental essence along dragon lines caused new mineral deposits to form in what we'd consider geological eyeblinks, but I can't swear to it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T04:12:32-07:00","id":1242911,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold?p=1242712#post1242712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The First Age wasn't a period of industrialization in the manner of our modern Earth.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean sure, we've devoured most of the low-hanging fruit in the space of a couple of centuries. But if the Old Realm mined at even a tenth of that pace, I still think they'd have exhausted most of what was in the modern-day threshold.<br /> <br /> You have a point about pacts with various spirits to supply ore, though. And Verzio has a point too. I forget the details, but I think I remember reading something about how Ahlat caused the jade at Bent Creek to form, and there was some sort of Celestial Audit over it.<br /> <br /> I reckon sorcerors could probably perform earthworks at a comparable rate to real-world industry, but they could probably get raw materials a dozen other ways too.<br /> <br /> Actually, the more I think about this, the more I feel like themes of depleted natural resources feel a bit wrong for Exalted. It's probably good that it can be addressed in so many other ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24421-katamari_naneke","name":"Katamari_Naneke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T08:17:22-07:00","id":1242949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Katamari_Naneke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold#post1242706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe there's an off-hand reference to the Bureau of Nature periodically renewing the ores of Creation somewhere, but if so I've missed it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Back during 1st edition there was a mention that Alhat had bribed the Goddesses of Jade into placing a massive deposit of Jade under Harborhead. Presumably the Jade Goddesses are allowed to renew the ores of Creation, but only in certain locations and at certain times, though it's possible for them to pick and choose other places if given the right incentive (and this is probably pretty illegal if they get caught doing it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T01:36:46-07:00","id":1244078,"title":"","content":"Right, right, that was the celestial audit I was thinking of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24421-katamari_naneke","name":"Katamari_Naneke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T02:23:09-07:00","id":1244086,"title":"","content":"Even without the intervention of gods, I'm personally inclined to view the substrata of Creation as relatively dynamic; minerals originate in the depths of the Elemental Pole of Earth, and tectonic and other earth processes carry them out across the plane and dredge them up to the surface over extended periods of time. It may be that the work of the Jadeborn assists in that process.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T09:31:21-07:00","id":1244177,"title":"","content":"We're still mining stuff on Earth.<br /> <br /> And Creation is waaaay bigger than Earth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T09:54:03-07:00","id":1244182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold?p=1244177#post1244177\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're still mining stuff on Earth.</div> </div> </div> </div>For how much longer?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And Creation is waaaay bigger than Earth.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe, but you've still got places close to the Inland Sea that haven't been tapped out yet (while still being close enough to the surface for mining techniques of the Age of Sorrows to access).<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T10:31:13-07:00","id":1244194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold?p=1244182#post1244182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> For how much longer?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Indefinitely?<br /> <br /> Depending on what you're mining for, and with new technologies emerging all the time, we haven't even scratched the surface of Earth's potential.<br /> <br /> For example, a few months back it was revealed that Japan has discovered an island which has sands super rich in rare earths, enough to supply our high tech production needs for over 700 years, once they figure out how to properly mine it.<br /> <br /> The other day I read a brazillian government report on iron deposits, there were many mines expected to last another 150 years, that is with current technology extraction rates.<br /> <br /> Imagine how long it would last with bronze age technology... It is for all practical senses interminable.<br /> <br /> There are mines that stay in operation for decades, again with extremely sofisticated post-industrial age extraction technologies being deployed.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T11:02:02-07:00","id":1244206,"title":"","content":"Why would you mine for anything besides magical materials when you have Wyld Cauldron technique, and you gan turn Ambrosia into Jade<br /> Make deals with gods in exchange for jade &gt; Use Jade to build anything you want. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12433-cruzwindt","name":"Cruzwindt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-10T07:29:28-07:00","id":1244483,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TGUEIROS</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold?p=1244194#post1244194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Indefinitely?</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Depending on what you're mining for, and with new technologies emerging all the time, we haven't even scratched the surface of Earth's potential.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">For example, a few months back it was revealed that Japan has discovered an island which has sands super rich in rare earths, enough to supply our high tech production needs for over 700 years, once they figure out how to properly mine it.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The other day I read a brazillian government report on iron deposits, there were many mines expected to last another 150 years, that is with current technology extraction rates.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Imagine how long it would last with bronze age technology... It is for all practical senses interminable.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">There are mines that stay in operation for decades, again with extremely sofisticated post-industrial age extraction technologies being deployed.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Suppose that in eight hundred years, we've extracted most stuff that's in the crust, and it would take some process for tapping minerals from the mantle, or cracking asteroids to keep up.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><i>Then</i> send people back to the Bronze Age.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200bThis subject has always been predicated on the question of how much would have already been extracted during the several thousand years of the First Age.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cruzwindt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold?p=1244206#post1244206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why would you mine for anything besides magical materials when you have Wyld Cauldron technique, and you gan turn Ambrosia into Jade<br /> Make deals with gods in exchange for jade &gt; Use Jade to build anything you want.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wyld Shaping magics aren't exactly convenient for these things.<br /> <br /> I comfortably assume that gods aren't generally inclined to share their ambrosia. It definitely seems odd to suggest that you can buy something that can be turned into jade in exchange for jade.<br /> <br /> Really, there's the rub; there are distinct limits to what you can give the gods in exchange for ambrosia that they can't just turn the ambrosia into.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-10T08:16:01-07:00","id":1244497,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1242706-mining-and-the-threshold?p=1244483#post1244483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Suppose that in eight hundred years, we've extracted most stuff that's in the crust, and it would take some process for tapping minerals from the mantle, or cracking asteroids to keep up.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><i>Then</i> send people back to the Bronze Age.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200bThis subject has always been predicated on the question of how much would have already been extracted during the several thousand years of the First Age.</span></span></span></div><br /> ...</div> </div> </div> </div>You reacted to someone saying &quot;we're still mining on Earth&quot; by saying &quot;for how much longer?&quot;<br /> <br /> What I'm saying is that depletion of mineral resources is not a current concern, even after all our extraction activity. We have centuries (if not millenia, once it becomes cost effective to prospect and mine the ocean floors) ahead of us on that matter. I would consider our civilization to have achieved a bittersweet victory if we can steer this ship long enough to experience depletion of our crust.<br /> <br /> There is no reason to assume that the First Age was as mining provided resources hungry as our current society. In the real world there is one way to go for a nation to grow, an that way goes through industrialization. Every single nation needs cars, trains, airplanes, ships, war machines, infrastructure...<br /> <br /> Creation's First Age had many kingdoms on many different paths. <br /> <br /> Magic allows things to be diverse, there could be whole nations that were considered militarily mighty without having mined an ounce of iron or smelted a steel bar.<br /> <br /> There could be cities full of glass towers that don't require a steel supporting structure.<br /> <br /> Continent sized landmasses where millions of people lived, being fed by magical fields (instead of modern agriculture, with it's trucks, tractors, harvesters, factories), who got around on baskets carried by giant turkeys (instead of cars, trains, airplanes and ships) who lived in ivory towers raised by sorcery (instead of modern housing and all it's concerns for engeneering and materials).<br /> <br /> So even if there were (and there were) mighty civilizations in which billions of people existed, it is just unimaginative and nitpicky to assume they all developed the same way and went after the same things (like in the real world), and so those things must have been exhausted by now. Like I said before, its magic, not reality.<br /> <br /> And that is besides the magical replenishment other people mentioned, which I don't think is necessary at all to be honest. Nothing I have read in the setting indicates mineral resources exhaustion to be a thing, or that it not being a thing somehow lacks verissimilitude.<br /> <br /> MIneral resources are plenty. It's abundancy or scarcity should only be a concern if it adds to the story being told.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2746-tgueiros","name":"TGUEIROS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2746_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1393"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}